      "math_academy_id": "e-4719",
      "problem": "Find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1},$ where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14138939/assets/5ee0df1e62e70438c45df032c9e0c02f7de26dc61defd92422efd9ca34ebe5d1.svg) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14138939/assets/a5934bf66ac9e935d938e533e65d8f712d40b994b9e42d4682efa787aad0056c.svg)",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_2^{-1}.$ We write down $E_2$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14138939/assets/433362b7b5868be5a49d7298e0366c1057a62fc4d282d2cd4c95b09bed86fbfd.svg)\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14138939/assets/0c9009beee636dbbd9ad32ebf1f82ef9b5c71698236cc8cf4010ef44b860b010.svg)",
